grammar => [
['Present perfect for the waiting that has happened', q{<em>"I interviewed on the 12th and have not heard back since"</em> — but the professional version stops at <em>"I interviewed on the 12th, and you mentioned feedback by Friday."</em> Present perfect ("have not heard") inserts your waiting into their face; the past-simple + their-promise version inserts only the facts. Same information, opposite temperature. Delete the have-not-heard sentence from every follow-up you write.}, q{"I interviewed on the 12th and have not heard back since"——而职业版本到"I interviewed on the 12th, and you mentioned feedback by Friday"为止。现在完成时（have not heard）把你的等待怼到对方面前；过去时 + 引用承诺的版本只放事实。信息相同，温度相反。把"至今未复"那句从每封跟进邮件里删掉。}],
['Indirect questions lower the temperature', q{<em>"Could you let me know whether the process is still on track?"</em> — the <strong>whether-clause</strong> makes the question about state, not about blame. Compare the direct "Did I fail to get the job?" Direct interrogatives demand verdicts; indirect whether-clauses request updates. Follow-ups live entirely in indirect territory.}, q{"Could you let me know whether the process is still on track?"——<strong>whether 从句</strong>让问题关于状态而非追责。对比直接问句"我是不是没戏了？"直接疑问句索要判决；间接 whether 从句请求更新。跟进邮件全程生存在间接语域里。}],
],
tips => [
q{<strong>Wait until their date passes</strong> — Friday promise means Monday email, not Friday morning. Patience first is what makes the email read calm.},
q{<strong>Subject line carries the role and date.</strong> You are one of twelve loops; make yourself findable in three seconds.},
q{<strong>Include the small ask.</strong> "Anything useful from my side?" converts your follow-up into their to-do item, which is why it gets answered.},
q{<strong>One follow-up, then five working days.</strong> A second message is a status check; a third without a reply is a decision about you — respect it and move energy elsewhere.},
q{<strong>Keep running other loops.</strong> The best follow-up tone comes from candidates who genuinely have alternatives. Build that, not just the email.},
],
practice => [
{ t => 'Write the four-sentence email', p => 'Subject line + four sentences: recall date, reference their timeline, status question with small ask, one-line interest. No sentence longer than 20 words.', a => q{Model: "Dear Priya — I interviewed for the Senior BA role on the 12th, and you mentioned feedback by Friday. Could you let me know whether the process is still on track, and whether anything from my side would be useful? References and documents are ready whenever helpful. The role remains very attractive to me. Best, [name]"} },
{ t => 'Temperature check', p => 'Which sentence is coldest, and why: (a) "I have not heard back since my interview." (b) "You mentioned feedback by Friday." (c) "I understand processes take time."', a => q{(a) — present perfect makes your waiting an accusation in present tense. (b) is neutral fact. (c) is warm but slightly patronising; use (b) plus a small ask instead. The coldest sentences are usually the truest ones — that is why they need the most care.} },
{ t => 'The silence plan', p => 'Draft your own cadence rules after sending the follow-up: when is your second message, what is in it, and what redirects your energy meanwhile?', a => q{Sensible default: five working days, then one final light message ("still very interested — happy to stay available"); meanwhile two new applications and one skills sprint per week. The plan exists so the silence has an owner: you.} },
],
},
