grammar => [
['Conditionals as negotiable futures', q{<em>"If the review becomes an intent in writing and the title lands at Senior, I am inclined to say yes."</em> — first conditional + present-tense willingness: <strong>if [verifiable condition], I am inclined to [outcome]</strong>. The conditions are checkable by tomorrow, which is what makes the sentence negotiation rather than hope. Never say "I am inclined to say yes" without its if-clause — that is a surrender.}, q{"If the review becomes an intent in writing and the title lands at Senior, I am inclined to say yes."——第一类条件句 + 现在时意愿：<strong>if [可验证条件], I am inclined to [结果]</strong>。条件明天就能核验，这才让这句话成为谈判而非许愿。不要在没有 if 从句的情况下说"I am inclined to say yes"——那是投降。}],
[' contrasts that price honesty', q{<em>"Intent, not a guarantee."</em>, <em>"The letter is the contract; the call is just its soundtrack."</em> — the <strong>X, not Y</strong> frame prices the difference between two similar words. Offer English is full of near-pairs: target/cap, intent/guarantee, base/package. Name the pair you are being given and you will never be surprised by your own payslip.}, q{"Intent, not a guarantee."、"The letter is the contract; the call is just its soundtrack."——<strong>X, not Y</strong> 句式为近义词之间的差异定价。Offer 英语充满近似对：target/cap、intent/guarantee、base/package。说出你拿到的是哪一对，你的工资单就不会给你惊喜。}],
],
