scenario => [
q{You spent <strong>eight years in accounting</strong> and are interviewing for a <em>Business Analyst</em> role in a bank\'s finance systems team. The interviewer has your CV open at the obvious place and asks the question you have rehearsed most: <em>"Why the switch from accounting to business analysis — and why should I believe it sticks?"</em>},
q{你做了 <strong>8 年会计</strong>，正在面试银行财务系统团队的 <em>Business Analyst</em> 岗位。面试官的简历就摊在最显眼的位置，问出你排练最多的问题：<em>"为什么从会计转业务分析——我凭什么相信你不会转回去？"</em>},
q{Career-change stories fail in one of two ways: <strong>apologising for the past</strong> or <strong>running from it</strong>. The winning shape does neither — it <strong>uses the past as the engine</strong>: the switch is not a rejection of accounting, it is the discovery that you liked the analysis inside accounting more than the accounting.},
q{转行故事的失败只有两种：<strong>为过去道歉</strong>或<strong>逃离过去</strong>。赢的结构两者都不做——它<strong>把过去变成引擎</strong>：转行不是否定会计，而是发现你喜欢的是会计里的"分析"，而不是会计本身。},
