grammar => [
['Past continuous for the drift, past simple for the moment', q{<em>"I was the accountant who ended up in the systems projects"</em> — past continuous paints the years-long drift into project rooms; <em>"Somewhere in page 20 I realised…"</em> — past simple marks the pivot moment. The tense system IS the story\'s shape: continuous for the pattern, simple for the decision. Career-change answers that blur the two sound either aimless or impulsive.}, q{"I was the accountant who ended up in the systems projects"——过去进行时描绘长达数年的"被卷入"；"Somewhere in page 20 I realised…"——一般过去时标记转折点。时态系统就是故事的形状：进行时讲模式，过去时讲决定。两者混用的转行回答，听起来要么漫无目的，要么一时冲动。}],
['Negative reframes: "not X — Y"', q{<em>"I did not leave accounting — I followed the part of it I was good at."</em> — the <strong>not-X, but-Y</strong> construction, delivered with the dash, replaces the listener\'s frame in one breath. Same engine: <em>"That is not a mid-life crisis; that is a pattern."</em> Career-change stories need two of these — one at the opening, one at the objection.}, q{"I did not leave accounting — I followed the part of it I was good at."——<strong>not-X, but-Y</strong> 结构 + 破折号，一口气替换听者的框架。同款引擎："That is not a mid-life crisis; that is a pattern."转行故事需要两句这种结构——开场一句，应对质疑一句。}],
],
tips => [
q{<strong>Never apologise for the years.</strong> Eight accounting years are your domain moat — the story is about redirecting them, not erasing them.},
q{<strong>Bring an artifact that survived.</strong> "48 pages, still in production" ends the capability debate before it starts.},
q{<strong>Split transfers into two buckets.</strong> Day-one skills (domain) and compounding skills (control thinking) — vague "transferable skills" convince nobody.},
q{<strong>Size the gap in quarters, not adjectives.</strong> "One-quarter learning curve" is checkable; "fast learner" is noise.},
q{<strong>Answer "will it stick" structurally.</strong> Market incentives plus prior evidence — willpower promises expire; structure does not.},
],
practice => [
{ t => 'Find your page 20', p => 'Identify the specific moment in your old career where the new one was already happening. Write it in two sentences with an artifact.', a => q{The moment must be concrete and dated — "during the ERP migration, in the reconciliation module spec". If you cannot find an artifact, manufacture evidence going forward: contribute to one open-source or internal project this quarter.} },
{ t => 'Bucket audit', p => 'List five skills from your old career. Sort them: day-one transferable, compounding transferable, not transferable. Kill the third bucket from your interview vocabulary.', a => q{The third bucket is where career changers waste airtime — "advanced Excel macros" means nothing to a banking BA panel unless framed as data-wrangling under audit constraints. Speak from buckets one and two only.} },
{ t => 'The sticks objection', p => 'Write your structural answer to "what stops you drifting back?" — one market reason, one evidence reason, zero promises.', a => q{Model: market — "transformation BA roles out-grow accounting at my level"; evidence — "I made this move internally three years ago and stayed". Notice both answers are about incentives and history — the two things interviewers can actually verify.} },
